WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.384
The binary fuse filter changes

2
00:00:02.454 --> 00:00:03.496
only one thing

3
00:00:03.496 --> 00:00:05.580
where a key's three slots go.

4
00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:08.895
The table is cut into many equal segments

5
00:00:08.895 --> 00:00:10.730
each a power of two long.

6
00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.499
A key picks a starting segment at random

7
00:00:13.558 --> 00:00:15.386
and its three slots lie in that

8
00:00:15.445 --> 00:00:16.920
segment and the next two.

9
00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:19.533
This layout comes from the fuse graphs

10
00:00:19.593 --> 00:00:21.320
of Dietzfelbinger and Walzer

11
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.630
from twenty nineteen.

12
00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:25.238
The library's public header sets the

13
00:00:25.294 --> 00:00:27.322
segment length and the slots per key

14
00:00:27.378 --> 00:00:28.730
from the number of keys.

15
00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.044
Ten thousand keys get segments

16
00:00:31.109 --> 00:00:32.745
of two hundred fifty-six

17
00:00:32.745 --> 00:00:35.560
and one point two five four slots per key.

18
00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.929
Two hundred thousand keys get

19
00:00:37.999 --> 00:00:39.670
one point one six seven.

20
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.103
A million keys get segments of eight

21
00:00:42.161 --> 00:00:44.013
thousand one hundred ninety-two

22
00:00:44.013 --> 00:00:45.460
and one point one three.

23
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.160
Is that enough?

24
00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:49.209
At two hundred thousand keys

25
00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:51.669
the header gives two hundred thirty-three

26
00:00:51.727 --> 00:00:54.070
thousand four hundred seventy-two slots.

27
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.100
We tried three seeds.

28
00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:58.039
An xor filter of that size

29
00:00:58.101 --> 00:00:59.384
got stuck every time

30
00:00:59.446 --> 00:01:01.341
while the same keys in the fuse

31
00:01:01.402 --> 00:01:02.930
layout peeled every time.

32
00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:04.779
Pause and predict.

33
00:01:04.849 --> 00:01:07.034
Ten thousand keys get one point

34
00:01:07.104 --> 00:01:09.078
two five four slots per key

35
00:01:09.148 --> 00:01:10.910
more than an xor filter's

36
00:01:10.981 --> 00:01:12.390
one point two three.

37
00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.180
Why would a small filter need more?

38
00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.689
The fuse layout's advantage grows

39
00:01:20.748 --> 00:01:22.410
with the number of segments.

40
00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:24.833
A small table has few of them

41
00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:27.320
so the header adds a safety margin.

42
00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:28.992
At a million keys

43
00:01:28.992 --> 00:01:32.150
slots per key fall to one point one three.

44
00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:35.150
Why is it called a fuse?

45
00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.219
The first segment can only

46
00:01:37.285 --> 00:01:38.872
hold a key's first slot

47
00:01:38.872 --> 00:01:41.318
and the last segment only its third

48
00:01:41.318 --> 00:01:43.500
so the two ends are thinly used.

49
00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:45.229
Peeling starts there

50
00:01:45.294 --> 00:01:47.395
and each removed key frees slots

51
00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:48.970
one segment further in

52
00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.530
like a fuse burning toward the middle.

53
00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:53.598
Here are two thousand keys

54
00:01:53.664 --> 00:01:54.920
in eleven segments.

55
00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:57.881
Each bar shows when its segment was peeled

56
00:01:57.881 --> 00:02:00.310
on average, as a percentage of the run.

57
00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.522
The end segments finish first

58
00:02:02.522 --> 00:02:04.321
at thirteen and six percent

59
00:02:04.383 --> 00:02:06.059
and the segments toward the

60
00:02:06.121 --> 00:02:07.300
middle finish last.

61
00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.092
A key's three slots are close together

62
00:02:10.149 --> 00:02:11.738
so the work stays in a small

63
00:02:11.794 --> 00:02:14.120
part of the table, which helps the cache.

64
00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:17.021
The paper reports construction more than

65
00:02:17.085 --> 00:02:19.190
twice as fast as for xor filters.

66
00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:22.900
So it needs fewer slots, and builds faster.

67
00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:24.440
To sum up.

68
00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:26.627
The three slots lie in three

69
00:02:26.692 --> 00:02:28.070
consecutive segments.

70
00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:30.275
The thin ends peel first

71
00:02:30.275 --> 00:02:32.500
and the peeling burns inward.

72
00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.002
At a million keys

73
00:02:34.066 --> 00:02:35.730
the filter needs one point

74
00:02:35.794 --> 00:02:37.330
one three slots per key.

75
00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:38.684
As an exercise

76
00:02:38.751 --> 00:02:41.160
try one point one zero slots per key

77
00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:43.168
at two hundred thousand keys

78
00:02:43.168 --> 00:02:44.640
and see what happens.

79
00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:48.270
Next, the task, and what the agents wrote.
